1. Test Scenario – High-level documentation of what to test
🔹 2. Test Case – Step-by-step instructions for executing a test
🔹 3. Test Suite – A collection of test cases grouped by functionality
🔹 4. Test Data – Input data used to execute test cases
🔹 5. Test Plan – A strategic document defining test scope, approach, and schedule
🔹 6. Requirement Traceability Matrix (RTM) – Maps test cases to requirements
🔹 7. Functional Testing – Tests the functionality of the software
🔹 8. Non-Functional Testing – Tests performance, usability, scalability, etc.
🔹 9. Exploratory Testing – Simultaneous learning, test design, and execution
🔹 10. Smoke Testing – Basic tests to verify build stability
🔹 11. Sanity Testing – Focused testing to verify bug fixes
🔹 12. Regression Testing – Ensuring new code changes don’t break existing features
🔹 13. Retesting – Testing a bug again after it has been fixed
🔹 14. Defect Life Cycle – The stages a bug goes through from discovery to closure
🔹 15. Severity – Impact of the bug on functionality
🔹 16. Priority – Urgency of fixing the bug
🔹 17. Boundary Value Analysis (BVA) – Testing at the boundaries of input ranges
🔹 18. Equivalence Partitioning – Dividing input into valid and invalid data sets
🔹 19. UAT (User Acceptance Testing) – Performed by end-users to validate requirements
🔹 20. QA (Quality Assurance) – Process-focused; ensures quality from the start
🔹 21. QC (Quality Control) – Product-focused; finds defects in the final output
🔹 22. Bug Leakage – Bugs missed by QA but found in production
🔹 23. Bug Release – Known bugs released intentionally with the product
🔹 24. Automation Testing – Scripts/tools to automate test execution
🔹 25. CI/CD – Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment for faster delivery
🔹 26. Assertions – Conditions to validate expected vs actual results
🔹 27. API Testing – Validating backend functionality using tools like Postman
🔹 28. Load Testing – Checking system performance under expected load
🔹 29. Stress Testing – Testing system limits under extreme load
🔹 30. Integration Testing – Ensuring multiple modules work together
🔹 31. End-to-End Testing – Testing the complete user journey
🔹 32. Maintainability – Ease of updating test scripts or framework
🔹 33. Test Coverage – Measure of how much of the application is tested
